  "client_view_text": " The write-ahead log is necessary because the operating system can crash or lose data before it can make sure that the database is updated.\nA write-ahead log is a file that is written to before any changes are made to the actual database. This allows the database to be sure that all changes made to the database are safely written to the log before the changes are actually made to the database itself. If the operating system crashes or loses data before the changes can be made to the database, the database can still recover from the crash by reading from the log and applying the changes to the database.\nFor example, suppose that the database is updating a record Here's a problem that illustrates a key concept:\n**Problem:** Design a database system that allows for online transaction processing; that is, it allows users to concurrently insert new records into the main data store. To do so, we will use a combination of the following: a main data store (which we will call the _on-disk_ data store), a write-ahead log (WAL), and a lock manager. The lock manager will be used to ensure that only one process can write to the main data store at a time. The WAL will be used to store the changes made by each transaction before they are written to the main data store.\n## Step 1: Describe the role of the write-ahead log (WAL) in ensuring database recovery after a crash.\nThe write-ahead log, or WAL, is a crucial component in ensuring database recovery after a crash. Its primary role is to store all changes made by transactions before they are written to the main data store. This means that every update operation is first recorded in the WAL, and then the main data store is updated accordingly. This approach ensures that the changes are preserved even if the database crashes or is shut down unexpectedly.\n\n## Step 2: Explain how the database recovers from a crash using the WAL"
}